State of New York } ss. County of Chenango }
On this 1 st day of November eighteen hundred and fifty one personally appeared before me the undersigned a Justice of the Peace duly authorized by law to administer oaths for general purposes within and for the County and State aforesaid Anna Breed a resident of the town of Lincklaen in the County and State aforesaid aged eighty one years who being duly sworn according to law doth on her oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provisions made by the law of the United States passed on the 1 st of July 1848. That she is a widow of Joseph Breed who was a Private in the Revolutionary war and who drew a Pension amounting to sixty Dollars per annum in conformity with the law of the United States passed June 7 th 1832. That she was married to the said Joseph Breed on the 12 th day of December 1786 in the town of Hillsdale Columbia county N.Y. by Israel Spencer a Justice of the Peace who has long since deceased and that she has made search and has not been able to find any record of their marriage and has every reason to believe that there is none. That her husband the said Joseph Breed died on the 12 th day of July 1850 in the town of Lincklaen in the county of Chenango and State aforesaid as will more fully appear by an affidavit made by the town Clerk which is herewith annexed. That she has surrendered her late husband's Certificate of Pension to the Agent for paying pensions at the City of Albany N.Y. and has drawn the back pay on the same, to wit, from the 4 th of March 1850 to the 12 th of July 1850 her
Sworn to and subscribed on the } Anna Y. Breed day and year first above written } mark.
before me
Elizabeth Fenton J. Justice of the Peace }

State of New York } County of Fulton } ss:
On this fifth day of October one thou- sand eight hundred and forty six, personally appeared, before me William L. Ingraham one of the Justices of the Peace, in and for the County aforesaid, Elizabeth Brown, of the town of Northamp- ton, State and County aforesaid, aged seventy six years, who, being first duly sworn according to Law, doth on her oath make the following declaration, in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the Act of Congress of June 17 th 1844, and by reason of the proviso in the Act of April 30 th 1844, entitled "An Act making appropriations for the payment of Revolutionary "and other Pensioners of the United States" &c. considered as a con- tinuation or extension of the Act of Congress of July 7 th 1838, en- titled "An Act granting half pay and pensions to certain widows": That she is the widow of Joseph Brown 2 d who was a pensioner of the United States at the rate of eighty dollars per annum under the act of Congress of June 7 th 1832.-
She further declares that she was married to the said Joseph Brown 2 d in the month of February one thousand seven hundred and eighty-eight, in the town of Cambridge, County of Washington, and State of New York, by a preacher of the Baptist order, whose name was William Wait: That her husband the aforesaid Joseph Brown 2 d died in the town of Northampton, State and County aforesaid on the twenty-ninth day of June, one thou- sand eight hundred and forty six, and that the arrears of Pension due at the time of his death, have been paid from the 4 th of March 1846, to the 29 th day of June 1846, in consequence of which the original certificate of Pension of the said Joseph Brown 2 d has been surrendered up. That she was not married to him prior to his leaving the service, but the marriage took place previous to the first of January seventeen hundred and ninety-four, viz: at the time above stated; therefore she hereby claims a Pension, (by virtue of the above recited Acts of Congress,) from the twenty ninth day of June one thousand eight hundred and forty six, until the day of her death;

State of New York } ss. Otsego County }
On this 31 st day of May A. D. 1861 personally appeared in open Court, before me Levi B. Turner, a judge in and for the County of Otsego, in the State of New York, Sarah Camp, of the town of Hartwick in Said County, aged seventy four years, being first duly sworn according to law, doth on her oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the law of the United States, passed on the 3 rd day of February A. D. 1853, that she is the widow of Benjamin Camp, who was a Marine in the Navy of the United States in the War of the Revolution, and served on board the Ship Providence, about the year 1778, and for said service, he received a pension certificate from the Government of the United States at the rate of Ninety Eight dollars per Annum, under the act passed by Congress on the 7 th day of June 1832, and for proof of said service, she refers the Commissioner of Pensions, to the proof on file in the pension office at Washington, in the case & application of her husband, Benjamin Camp for a pension, and also, a certificate of pension issued to him by the department, of which the following is a true copy. War Department. Revolutionary Claim. I certify that in conformity with the law of the United States of the 7 th of June 1832. Benjamin Camp, of the State of New York, who was a Marine in the Navy of the Revolution, is entitled to receive Ninety Eight dollars per Annum, during his natural life, commencing on the 4 th of March 1831.
(over)

State of Tennessee } S.S. County of Davidson }
On this 15 th day of April A.D. 1851, personally appeared, before me Thomas Maney , Judge of the 6 th Judicial Circuit, of the State of Tennessee , Allen Cotton , Admin- istrator of Priscilla Cotton dec d , and made oath in due form of law, that he is the Administrator of said Priscilla Cotton , who was the Widow of Thomas Cotton deceased , who was a Captain in the North Carolina Line , Army of the Revolution , and who died in Sumner County, Tennessee , many years ago, that he left a Widow, Priscilla Cotton aforesaid , who survived him until on or about the 1 st day of November A.D. 1843 , when she died, that since her death an application was made by her then surviving children, through her (then) Administrator Josiah Malton , for a Pension, which was allowed, and a Certificate of Pension was issued in her name, under Act of Congress of July 2 nd 1836 , at the rate of Two Hundred Dollars per annum, said Pension commencing on the 2 nd day of March A.D. 1831 , and terminating on the 1 st day of November A.D. 1843 , being the day of her death, (as declarant believes); That Declarant is informed, and believes, that the amount then allowed, was not as much as they were entitled to under the aforesaid Act of July 2 nd 1836 , and that he makes this Declaration for the purpose of obtaining an Increase of Pension under that Act, Declarant having always understood from his Mother, Priscilla Cotton aforesaid , that her husband, the aforesaid Thomas Cotton , served as a Captain for several years during the Revolutionary War , but Declarant can not of course state the precise duration of his service; That at the time, that the above mentioned application for Pension was made, the then surviving children of the said Thomas, and Priscilla Cotton , were all properly identified, and that, as Declarant supposes, the proofs of their identity is now on file, in the Pension Office, at Washington City, together with the proof of the marriage of the said Thomas, and Priscilla Cotton , all of which papers, Declarant believes, were forwarded by N. Fergusson, Esq.
(and)

State of New Hampshire } County of Cheshire }
On this 17 th day of May A.D. 1853. Personally appeared before the Probate Court of the County of Cheshire State of New Hampshire Susan ay Hills a resident of Alstead in the county of Cheshire and State aforesaid, aged Fifty five years, who first being duly sworn according to law, doth make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the act of Congress passed on the 3 rd February 1853, granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary war; that she is the widow of James de Saxon, who was a Private in the Army of the Revolution, for which he was granted a pension under the Law of eighteen hundred and thirty two and inscribed on the Roll of New Hampshire agency at Concord N.H. at the rate (I believe) $40.40 per annum payable on the 4 th of March and 4 th September, that his Certificate was surrendered to the agent at Concord N.H. soon after his death; You are Respectfully referred to the evidence on file office with his application for a pension in the Pension for the Justice of my claim.
She further declares that she was Married to the said James Saxon at Marlow county of Cheshire and State of New Hampshire on the twenty fifth day of December eighteen hundred and twenty five; That he died at Washington New Hampshire on the thirtieth day of March eighteen hundred and forty two, leaving her a widow, that she was not Married to him prior to the second of January eighteen hundred, but at the time

State of North Carolina } ss County of Surry }
On this 5th day of March 1856, personally appeared before me, a Justice of the peace, within and for the County and State aforesaid Mary Foddell aged 79 years, a resident of the County and State aforesaid, who, being duly sworn according to law, declares that she is the widow of Charles Foddell, who served in the Company Commanded by Captain — in the war of the Revolution and continued in actual service in said war for at least 14 days and was honorably discharged &c. She further states that she was married to the said Charles Foddell in the County of Patrick, State of Virginia on the day of and that her name before her said marriage was Mary Nelson that her said husband died in Patrick County Virginia on the day of 18 and that she is now a widow.
She further declares that her said husband drew a pension in his life time at the rate of ninety six dollars per annum - and that she now refers to his declaration filed in Washington City for proof of his service. She further declares that she has made application for Pension under act of 3 Feby. 1853, and obtained Certificate of Pension at the rate of ninety six dollars per annum recorded in the Pension Office on the Roll of Pensions under act Feby 3d, 1853, Page 123. Vol A. (to which declaration and Certificate she now refers

