State of Kentucky } S.S. County of Allen }
On this 12 th day of August A.D. 1868. Before me John B. Collins, clerk of the county court, a court of record, within and for said county, personally appeared Mrs. Martha Giles, a resident of said county, aged about 113 years, to me personally known, who being by me first duly sworn according to law, doth, on her oath, make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the Joint resolution of Congress of February 18, 1867, granting increased rate of pension to certain widows of soldiers and others of the revolutionary war.
That she is the widow of John Giles, who, was a soldier in said war from near its commencement until its termination, as she well remembers, but as to the particular service he rendered throughout said war she is unable to state, or the names of the officers under whom he served, but he enlisted as a private in said service in the county of Rowan in the State of North Carolina, and for and on account of his said services, he applied for and obtained a pension until his death, which occurred in this Allen county on or about the 12 th day of September 1852, and that she was married to the said John Giles in the county of Rowan in the State of North Carolina by one George Read a Justice of the Peace, but a short time before her said husband enlisted in the service aforesaid, but does not em remember the date of her said marriage, and has no record of it, and for the proof of her said husband's service in said revolutionary war, and her inter-marriage with him, and when it took place, she hereby refers to the papers on file in her original application for a pension, and those filed in the application of her deceased husband, and she further declares, that she is the same person in whose favor a certificate of pension was issued on the 7 th day of August A.D. 1855, by the secretary of the Interior, at the rate of Eighty dollars per annum, commencing on the 12 th day of September 1852, and which pension has been paid her to

State of Virginia County of Amherst }
Before me Zack D'Ansley — a Justice of the Peace within and for the county and State aforesaid, on this eighth day of December 1855, personally appeared James Grant — and Patrick H Grant of Amherst county, Virginia, of lawful age, to me well known to be persons of veracity, and whose testimony is entitled to full faith and credit, who having been duly sworn according to law, depose as follows:
That they are neighbors, and are well acquainted with Susanna Grant, widow of Robert Grant, deceased, and with her husband in his life time; that they lived together as man and wife from the first of their recollection until his death, and raised a large family of children, who are recognized as such, and that they never heard it questioned or denied by anyone that they were legally married; that the said Robert Grant, her husband, was in receipt of a pension as a Revolutionary soldier, for twenty dollars per annum, and that they have seen the certificate of pension; that the said Robert Grant died in Amherst county, Virginia on the 20th day of July, 1838, and that Susanna Grant is his widow, not having been married since his death. That they are in no wise interested in this claim for a pension for Susanna

State of Pennsylvania } Dauphin County SS }
On the fifth day of May A.D. One thousand eight hundred and fifty four personally appeared before the Subscribers one of the Judges of the Court of Common Pleas (a Court of Record) for the County of Dauphin Mary Gray, a resident of Swatara Township in the County of Dauphin and State of Pennsylvania, aged Eighty three years, who being duly sworn according to Law, doth on her oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provisions made by the Act of Congress passed July 29. 1848 & its supplements viz - That she is the Widow of Robert Gray, late of the County and State aforesaid, who was a private and Ensign in the Army of the Revolution, and as such received in his lifetime a certificate of pension from the United States, bearing date the 25 th of February A.D. 1833 in the sum of Forty four \frac{8}{100} Dollars per annum, as by the said original certificate in her possession, and purporting to be recorded at the proper office in Washington City in "Book D. Vol. 6 page 59" well more fully appear. She further declares that she was married to the said Robert Gray on the day of June - in the year of our Lord Seventeen hundred and ninety, but that she has no record proof of her said marriage, nor does she know of any person now living who was present at, and can testify to the fact of the said marriage, and she is therefore obliged to transmit to the Department, as the best evidence in her power, the depositions of some of the oldest inhabitants of the neighbourhood who testify their knowledge in regard to the matter; that her Husband the aforesaid Robert Gray, died on the twenty seventh day of April A.D. Eighteen hundred and forty eight; that she was not married to him prior to his leaving the service but the marriage took place previous to the Second of January A.D. Eighteen hundred viz. at or about the time above stated. She further states that she is now a widow, and has been such ever since the death of her said Husband; and that she has never before made any application for a pension.
Sworn & subscribed the day & year above written, before me } Mary I do hereby certify that the said Mary Gray cannot } by reason of bodily infirmity attend the Court. }
J. A. Heister Judge
Mary Gray
